            'title' => "Digitalization doesn't start with a big project",
            'excerpt' => 'It starts with the one routine that costs hours every week — the one nobody but you can see.',
            'meta' => 'Digitalization for small businesses does not begin with new software for the whole company, but with a single workflow. Three questions that find the right place to start.',
            'tags' => 'digitalization, workflows, small-business',
            'published' => '2026-08-04 09:00:00',
            'body' => "When digitalization comes up, most people picture something large: new software for the whole company, training, migration, months of disruption. That is off-putting for good reason. It is also, usually, the wrong place to start.\n\nThe better start is smaller and far less dramatic. It sits with the one task that eats time every week and that nobody complains about any more, because everyone has got used to it.\n\n## Three questions that find the start\n\n**Which number do you write down more than once?** A price that lives in the till, in a price list, and again on the website. A quantity that moves from a delivery note into a spreadsheet and from there onto an invoice. Every place the same information is entered a second time is a place where the two can drift apart.\n\n**What do you regularly have to ask somebody else?** If you can't look up yourself how much is left or when something was delivered, then a piece of information is attached to a person rather than to a place. That works for exactly as long as the person is there.\n\n**Which task do you keep putting off?** Procrastination is a reliable sign that something is needlessly awkward. Not unimportant — awkward.\n\nThe answer to one of those questions is your starting point. Not the software that can do the most.\n\n## Why starting small is the faster route\n\nA small start has three advantages a large project doesn't.\n\nIt is **quick to undo**. If it turns out after four weeks that the solution doesn't fit, you have lost four weeks and not a year.\n\nIt **proves something**. After the first change you no longer know in theory but concretely whether digital workflows save time in your business. That is an entirely different basis for the next decision.\n\nIt **pulls the rest along**. Almost always, the first workflow reveals that the second comes along nearly for free on the same foundation. Get your product data into one place properly and you have half-solved the online shop, the price list and the stocktake.\n\n## What a good first step costs\n\nConsiderably less than most people expect — precisely because it isn't the whole business. A well-built template, a small capture tool, or a report that pulls three sources together is often a matter of a few days.\n\nThe real effort rarely sits in the technology. It sits in understanding the workflow properly first: who does what, in what order, and why that way? Answering that honestly is the part that makes the eventual solution fit.\n\n## Where it isn't worth it\n\nThat matters too. A workflow you go through twice a year doesn't justify its own solution, however irritating it is. Something that only affects you and that you're fast at, likewise. And where an existing off-the-shelf product hits eighty per cent of the problem, that is often better than a bespoke one that hits a hundred per cent and has to be maintained.\n\nThe question is never \"can this be done digitally?\" — it almost always can. The question is whether the effort pays for itself within a sensible period.\n\n## The next step\n\nSpend a week paying deliberate attention to where you enter the same information a second time. Usually that is enough to find the start.\n\nIf you like, tell me in two sentences what you noticed. I'll tell you honestly whether building something is worth it — and if it isn't, that too.",
